    "wordCount": 825,
    "text": "Inside the station concourse, overhead fluorescent tubes fought against dusty afternoon glare slanting down the stairwell. Tinny recorded chimes echoed above the turnstiles, instantly swallowed by the heavy pneumatic hiss and squealing steel brakes of a southbound local train pulling out from platform two. Near the automatic fare bank, an older salaryman in a wrinkled grey suit stood tapping the glass touch panel twice with a rolled newspaper, muttering steadily at a coin slot that kept spitting his hundred-yen piece back into the metal tray.\n\nI bypassed the automated machines and stepped up to the manned glass window beside the wide side gate.\n\nBetween us sat thick acrylic glass webbed with faint horizontal wiping scratches from years of sleeve cuffs and paper tickets. When I slid a thousand-yen note beneath the curved metal slot and named **Soma Station** clearly, the station clerk paused mid-reach above the roll of magnetic blanks.\n\nHis eyes lifted from my fingers to my collar, then lingered a full second longer on my face before dipping politely back down to the ticket tray. It took me half a second to understand why—the combination of the long dark-teal hair brushing my sweater collar, the smooth pale jawline, and the soft chest tone carrying across the scratched glass made him glance twice to match the face against the voice.\n\n\"Four stops past the river transfer,\" he said evenly, punching the blue fare stamp without commenting. A tiny **magnetic paper ticket** clicked cleanly out of the slot alongside crisp change. \"Platform one, express doesn't stop there—take the local on the inner track.\"\n\n\"Platform one, local. Thank you.\" I scooped the small stiff paper rectangle into my palm, tucked the coins back inside the worn leather wallet, and slipped through the mechanical barrier gate cleanly without bumping the red flap.\n\nBy the time the inner-track train pulled alongside the yellow safety tiles, the carriage wasn't packed wall-to-wall, just full enough that every vinyl bench held coats, school satchels, and folded umbrellas. I claimed an open corner beside the sliding glass doors, hooked my fingers around the cold brushed-steel handrail, and pressed the folded paper map flat against my chest.\n\nThe doors thumped shut. Electric motors whined low beneath the floorboards as the carriage slid smoothly out from under the concrete canopy into blinding afternoon light.\n\nNeighborhood roofs dropped below track level almost immediately. Concrete blocks packed tight beside narrow drainage alleys loosened into single-family homes with neat tiled walls, small potted citrus trees wedged beside concrete driveways, and rows of drying laundry snapping softly in the wind from second-floor balconies. Telephone wires dipped and rose past the glass in crisp black arcs, rhythmic against low clouds and slate shingles. Twice, the railway curved out across open elevated embankments where pale green grass and river reeds widened the horizon cleanly for three quiet minutes before brick apartments closed back in around the rails.\n\nSun struck across the scratched window glass from high southwest, turning tiny hairline scuffs into bright silver threads across the moving city while ghosts of seated commuters hung translucent over the rushing utility poles.\n\nStanding still against the cold steel pole, with nothing left to steer or check for ten straight minutes, the quiet inside my chest began pulling inward again.\n\nI looked down at the pale fingers wrapped around the paper map and felt the bottom drop softly out of my stomach. Back home, someone was probably unlocking a front door right around this hour in a completely different time zone—or maybe not, maybe it was still midnight there, quiet kitchen tiles cooling under dark cabinets while an unanswered phone sat dark on an entry table. My dead Panasonic sat cold inside my pocket like a smooth flat stone. No contacts. No text history that belonged to me. Not a single voice I recognized within three thousand miles of this carriage floor.\n\nThe train plunged suddenly into a long concrete cut-and-cover tunnel beneath a hill road.\n\nInstantly, every sunlit neighborhood outside blinked black, and the scratched door glass snapped flat into a hard black mirror six inches from my nose.\n\nThere he was again—Ira Lowe's pale, fine-boned face floating inside the dark tunnel reflection beneath yellow ceiling fluorescents, green eyes steady and wide, dark strands blown slightly loose across his temple.\n\nI held my own gaze across six inches of cold glass while the carriage rattled loud over rail joints.\n\n*Not today,* I told the reflection silently, jaw tightening until the dull ache behind my eyes steadied. Break down over every crossroads between here and home and I wouldn't make it past tomorrow's front desk. One task first. Find the building, trace the gate route, sleep tonight, take the test.\n\nSteel rails screamed softly on a gentle curve, and daylight slammed bright back through the windows as the carriage broke clear into open air above a wide tiled station concourse. The overhead signboard slid past cleanly in crisp black lettering: **SOMA STATION**.\n\n***"
