    "eventAnchors": "Arrival & Navigation",
    "wordCount": 867,
    "text": "Soma wasn't a quiet sleeping suburb tucked behind pine ridges. Stepping out through the main east turnstiles felt like walking straight into a full, bustling municipal center with weight of its own.\n\nThe station apron poured commuters outward beneath high glass awnings toward wide paved boulevards, lined on both sides with multi-storey brick storefronts, stationery shops, mid-rise office blocks, delivery vans blinking amber hazards at loading bays, and dense tangles of bicycles packed wheel-to-wheel along silver guardrails. Afternoon sunlight poured sharp down the center of the avenue, cutting deep cool shadow beneath the western awnings while baking warmth straight off the crosswalk stripes.\n\nTwo yards past the stone ticket steps, I stopped out of the main foot traffic and snapped the stiff paper map open again.\n\nSomeone clipped my right shoulder hard enough to spin my heel an inch on the concrete.\n\n\"Sorry—!\" I started instinctively, grabbing the fluttering paper edge before it tore.\n\n\"Sorry,\" a deep voice clipped back beside me.\n\nA tall office worker in a charcoal wool suit stepped cleanly sideways to recover his stride, dark hair combed back without a loose strand, one hand locked tight around a heavy dark leather briefcase. He barely spared a distracted half-nod over his suit collar before the crosswalk stream swallowed him cleanly back toward the bus island.\n\nI smoothed the crumpled corner of the map back flat, picked the widest northward avenue marked along the municipal grid, and set off at a steady pace.\n\nTen minutes later, past a flower stand and an electronics shop stacking silver rice cookers beside its door, the road curved gently westward under dense plane trees. Five minutes after that, I passed a corner mailbox with a crooked blue sticker, stopped three paces clear of a pedestrian island, and stared dead ahead across the street.\n\nThe exact same grey vending machine with a chipped red corner badge sat twenty feet opposite a parked white delivery van.\n\n\"No,\" I muttered quietly to myself, lowering my arms until the stiff paper sagged between both hands. I checked behind me. Checked the street nameplate nailed above the corner bank. Checked the crooked map crease again. \"That's the exact same corner.\"\n\n*Ring-ring.*\n\nA brass bicycle bell chimed sharply four feet behind my left elbow. I stepped smartly onto the concrete curb just as a heavy steel commuter bike rolled smoothly past and braked softly three yards ahead with a faint rubber squeak.\n\nA teenage girl stayed balanced cleanly on one sneaker tip against the asphalt, wearing an unbuttoned dark school blazer over a neat cream sweater, her dark ponytail swinging forward over one shoulder as she tilted her head back toward me.\n\n\"You okay?\" she asked, glancing smoothly down from my face to the wide-open map pinned against my ribs.\n\n\"Yeah,\" I said, holding up the creased corner weakly. \"Just apparently walking in tight circles.\"\n\n\"Looking for somewhere?\"\n\n\"Soma Academy.\"\n\nHer eyebrows lifted half an inch with easy recognition, nodding once toward the upper street behind her handlebars. \"Oh. Yeah, you blew right past the turn back by the post office.\"\n\nI stared down at the black street grid under my thumb. \"Seriously? Again?\"\n\nShe let out a quick, bright laugh, kicking her pedal half a turn upward to rest her heel. \"Yeah. Again. Everyone misses it the first week—the main boulevard bends slowly to the left after the bank, so if you follow the wide pavement straight, it loops you right back onto this cross-street.\"\n\n\"Apparently I'm great at finding the long way back to the same vending machine.\"\n\n\"You're really not,\" she teased lightly, eyes crinkling as a small grin broke across her face. \"Sorry—no, seriously, it's a sneaky corner.\"\n\nI shook my head once, a quiet laugh slipping loose before my chest tightened back up. \"No, you're right. It's fine.\"\n\nShe lifted one gloved hand off her handlebar grip and pointed cleanly back the way I'd come—two blocks down past the white van. \"Go back past the mailbox to that four-way light. There's a corner bakery with a striped green awning right across from the dry cleaner's. Cut left straight up the stone hill beside the bakery wall and just keep walking straight.\"\n\nI squinted back down the sunlit boulevard until I caught the edge of a dark green canvas awning two blocks away. \"And that takes me to the gates?\"\n\n\"Eventually,\" she said evenly, completely matter-of-fact without a trace of sarcasm.\n\nI blinked at her for two seconds. \"That's not overwhelmingly reassuring.\"\n\nShe laughed bright and clear, pushing off cleanly on her right pedal as the pedestrian light shifted green ahead. \"You'll make it! Just stop trusting the big road!\"\n\n\"Thanks!\" I called after her.\n\nShe lifted one hand straight up without turning her head, dark ponytail swaying cleanly as her bike merged neatly into the afternoon bicycle lane and vanished around the corner traffic.\n\nI stood on the curb for two quiet breaths, folded the stubborn map cleanly along its third crease, and pushed off back down toward the green bakery awning. At least the city hadn't swallowed me whole yet. Maybe I just needed a better sense of north—or a map that didn't fold straight over the crucial turn.\n\n***"
