    "wordCount": 867,
    "text": "Past the green-awned bakery, Soma climbed gently upward through warmer residential streets that grew wider, quieter, and steadily greener with every hundred yards.\n\nStone retaining walls replaced brick storefronts, topped cleanly with trimmed hedges and pale plaster eaves. More teenagers began filling the sidewalks now—pairs walking home with leather school bags slung low off one shoulder, three boys arguing loudly over an arcade score while balancing iced drinks in plastic bags, a girl on a step-through bicycle steering carefully one-handed with an overflowing wicker basket packed full of fresh yellow flowers.\n\nHalfway up the long paved incline, my throat felt dry as chalk from walking fast in warm spring wind. I turned aside under the bright fluorescent hum of a corner convenience store, grabbed a cold canned peach tea from the chilled glass case, and set exact yen coins onto the rubber counter mat.\n\nThe teenage clerk at the register punched the key cleanly, looked up to hand back my receipt—and froze mid-motion for a full second, eyes dropping quickly from my pale face down to the heavy black-and-teal hair framing my collar before clearing his throat awkwardly and bowing slightly too fast.\n\nI took the cold aluminum can without comment, murmured thanks, and pushed back through the sliding glass doors into the sunlight.\n\nRight as I popped the ring tab, a brisk spring gust tore straight down between two stone garden walls and whipped half my loose hair straight across my mouth and eyes in a tangled dark cloud.\n\n\"Seriously?\" I snapped through a mouthful of cold black strands, clamping the aluminum can under my elbow so both hands could rake the mess back behind my ears.\n\nA couple of uniformed high-school girls walking past ten feet away caught the tail end of it; one clapped a hand hastily over her mouth, choking back a bright startled laugh before dragging her friend faster toward the corner crossing without looking back twice.\n\nI smoothed the stray locks behind my collar, popped the tea open cleanly, and swallowed half the cold sweet can in three pulls before resuming the climb. Even getting blindsided by wind on a public sidewalk felt strangely grounding. The avenue around me hummed with ordinary, loud spring life—a baker in flour-dusted trousers shouting a quick joke across the lane to an old man sweeping maple leaves off his stone step, mothers calling small children back from scooter driveways, glass windows gleaming clean from freshly washed sills. Nobody was hiding behind shuttered curtains. The air itself carried warmth, noise, and easy motion that didn't demand anything back except walking cleanly along the curb.\n\nThen the stone wall on my right broke open onto a wide paved forecourt, and I stopped dead with the half-empty tea can resting against my palm.\n\nBeyond heavy wrought-iron gates set between twin carved stone gateposts, **Soma Academy** rose cleanly across a wide rise of trimmed cedar and paved walkways.\n\nIt was far larger than a folded sheet of heavy stationery had let me picture.\n\nBroad brick-and-stone facades stood anchored back behind sweeping gravel and flagstones, dark slate rooflines stepped cleanly against the pale blue spring sky, and tall rowed classroom windows framed orderly green courtyards disappearing out of sight behind older western wings. Afternoon sun struck the hill low and warm across the stonework, throwing long sharp cedar shadows across the entrance drive while lighting up every upper-floor pane along the main building simultaneously—flaring bright gold-white across the glass until I had to narrow my eyes and raise one hand slightly above my brow to keep looking.\n\nAcross the broad courtyard inside, students in crisp dark uniforms crossed slowly between low arched walkways, scattered voices drifting faint and bright across the open drive. The entire campus carried an old, quiet solidity—not flashy wealth, just the settled weight of stone that had stood watching seasons roll across Soma long before Jonah Laurence ever stepped off a street at home.\n\nFor the first time since opening my eyes to pre-dawn grey glass, my thoughts weren't turning inward toward concrete roads back home, or silent kitchens, or even the pale stranger waiting inside bathroom mirrors. My fingers closed steady around the cool metal can, watching light slide down dark slate eaves.\n\nSomewhere deep inside the main courtyard, an electric bell rang three clean silver chimes across the trees.\n\nHalf a dozen students near the lower portico picked up their pace toward the side steps, laughing softly across their shoulders. Wind stirred cool cedar needles along the upper fence line.\n\nTomorrow morning at eight o'clock, I would walk straight between those stone gateposts with a pencil in my pocket and an Applicant No. 1044 slip folded neatly inside Ira Lowe's worn leather wallet.\n\nI stood for ten quiet seconds watching upper-story glass flash clean gold above the cedars, then set the empty aluminum can inside the corner recycling bin beside the bus bench.\n\nI reached into my jacket pocket, flattened the stiff paper transit map carefully along its worn seams one final time until every fold lay square and neat beneath my thumb, and slid it away deep inside my coat.\n\nTomorrow I would take the exam.\n\nToday, I knew where the gates stood."
